<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Bolsena</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsena">Bolsena</a> is a crater lake in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the largest lake of volcanic origin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In Etruscan times its shores belonged to the territory of Volsinii, and the federal sanctuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fanum-voltumnae">Fanum Voltumnae</a>, lay nearby. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the commune of Orvieto extended its rule towards it, and the Farnese Duchy of Castro later covered the lands between the lake and the Tyrrhenian coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-bolsena.jpg" alt="Lake Bolsena" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jackyd101 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Bolsena_from_Montefiascone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsena">Bolsena</a> lies in the northern part of the province of Viterbo, called Alto Lazio or Tuscia. It is the largest volcanic lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Roman records indicate that the Vulsini volcano was active as recently as 104 BCE, and it has been dormant since. The lake formed when a circular area collapsed after the magma chamber that fed the volcanic district was depleted, and the two islands in the south were formed by underwater eruptions following that collapse. It has an oval shape, with its long axis running north to south, and a roughly conical bed. The Marta River is its only outlet and flows to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a> near Lido di Tarquinia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called the lake Lacus Volsinii, adapting the Etruscan name Velzna, that of the last Etruscan city to hold out against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, whose people were moved after 264 BCE; its original site has not been securely identified. A modernised Roman consular road, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-cassia">Via Cassia</a>, runs along one side. One third of the lake was donated to the Church by the noble Alberici family of Orvieto, who were honoured with a ceremony three times a year performed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Orvieto.</p><p>The island of Bisentina, the largest at 17 hectares, gave refuge from Saracen raids in the 9th century. About 1250 it passed to the lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bisenzio">Bisenzio</a>, who burned it after disputes with its inhabitants. Urban IV reconquered it in 1261, and in 1333 Louis IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> destroyed it. It belonged to the Farnese family from 1400 and was visited by many popes. Its monuments include a church with a cupola by Jacopo Barozzi da Vignola and a small temple of Saint Catherine by Antonio da Sangallo. On Martana, it is said, the Ostrogothic queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalasuntha">Amalasuntha</a> was assassinated, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> used as a reason for war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The watershed had 22,000 permanent residents in 2004 and 35,000 in summer. Eight comuni stand on the shore: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsena">Bolsena</a>, Montefiascone, Marta, Capodimonte, Valentano, Gradoli, Grotte di Castro and San Lorenzo Nuovo, which was moved from an older site further down the slope to escape malaria from marshes on the northern shore. Etruscan antiquities remain at the old site. Orvieto lies about 14 kilometres to the north-east. Each shore comune has a stretch of beach, and the surroundings hold camping and farm-stay establishments.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Bolsena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The commune extended its rule over much of the surrounding countryside towards <b>Lake Bolsena</b> and the Tuscan Maremma.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-orvieto">Commune of Orvieto</a></li><li><blockquote>It covered the old Farnese estates between <b>Lake Bolsena</b> and the Tyrrhenian coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-castro">Duchy of Castro</a></li><li><blockquote>Volsinii controlled a rich territory around <b>Lake Bolsena</b> and traded widely, as imported Greek pottery shows.</blockquote><a href="/state/volsinii">Volsinii</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 7th and 6th centuries BCE it had become one of the richest cities of Etruria, importing luxury goods and controlling a large territory reaching towards <b>Lake Bolsena</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/vulci">Vulci</a></li></ul></section>
